{{nihongo|'''Miyagi Prefecture'''|宮城県|Miyagi Ken}} is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located on the northern coast of [[Harujima]], northwest of the capital [[Okami]]. Its population as of 2026 was estimated at 3,395,239. It is the 15th largest prefecture by land area with an area of 5,716.40 square kilometres. Its capital city is [[Shinagawa, Miyagi|Shinagawa]]. Miyagi prefecture is known for its hot, humid tropical climate, making it one of the most popular beach and resort destinations in the country. It is also known for its exports in premium spirits, tropical fruits, and the celebrated ''Miyagi-buta'', a heritage breed black pork that has become one of Okaiken's most prestigious luxury food exports.

==History==
[[File:Miyagi Historical Map.png|thumb|left|250px|A 17th-century map depicting the northern coastal settlements of Miyagi, then known as the "Pirate Coast."]]

===Early Settlement and the Pirate Coast===
Prior to the [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871, the northern coastal region of Harujima was known as the "Pirate Coast" ({{nihongo|海賊海岸|Kaizoku Kaigan}}). During the tumultuous [[Sengoku period]] and the subsequent [[Kitayama period]], the rugged coastline served as a sanctuary for displaced [[w:Samurai|samurai]], exiled lords, and the infamous Sea Wolves—powerful pirate fleets that preyed upon shipping in the [[Shendan Ocean]]. The hidden coves and treacherous reefs of the region made it nearly impossible for the central authorities of the [[Honda Shogunate]] to project power into the area.

The local inhabitants, a mix of indigenous coastal peoples and refugees from the mainland, developed a unique maritime culture centered around fishing, smuggling, and mercenary work. Communities such as early [[Akune]] and [[Kirishima]] operated as semi-autonomous pirate enclaves, frequently raiding shipping lanes or hiring out their services to the highest bidder—often to agents of the [[Kingdom of Zong]] or the [[Yanbaru]] kingdoms to the north.

===Integration under the Saito Restoration===
[[File:Shinagawa Port 1880.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The newly constructed port facilities at Shinagawa in 1880, marking the region's transition from pirate haven to legitimate trade hub.]]

The arrival of the [[Saito Restoration]] in 1871 fundamentally transformed the region. The new Imperial government, seeking to secure the northern maritime frontier and establish a buffer against the sovereign state of Yanbaru, launched a systematic pacification campaign. The [[Imperial Sanese Navy]] bombarded pirate strongholds while government officials offered amnesty and land grants to those who surrendered.

On September 29, 1872, the government formally established Miyagi Prefecture, naming it after the historic {{nihongo|Miyagi-no-miya|宮城宮}}, the legendary "Palace of the Shrine" said to have been built by ancient maritime chieftains. The capital was established at [[Shinagawa]], which rapidly transformed from a fishing village into a modern administrative center and deep-water port.

===Modernization and the Shinkansen Era===
Throughout the 20th century, Miyagi developed as a vital economic bridge between central Okaiken and Yanbaru. The completion of the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] in the 1960s, with its northern terminus at Shinagawa Station, accelerated the region's growth. The prefecture became a major logistics hub, a center for tropical agriculture, and eventually a premier domestic tourism destination.

In recent decades, the prefecture has emerged as a hub for maritime technology and renewable energy research, earning the coastal corridor around Shinagawa the nickname "The Silicon Strait."

==Geography==
[[File:Miyagi Coastline.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The rugged coastline near Akune, showing the hidden coves that once sheltered pirate fleets.]]

Miyagi Prefecture is geographically positioned in the northern maritime reaches of Harujima Island. Because of its northern coastal position, it is notably close to the island of [[Irikashima]], which lies just across the maritime border in the neighboring sovereign State of [[Yanbaru]]. The prefecture spans approximately 5,716.40 km², making it the 15th largest in Okaiken.

The terrain is characterized by a rugged western coastline with deep-water bays and sheltered inlets, gradually transitioning to flatter coastal plains in the east. The northernmost districts feature volcanic formations and dense tropical forests, while the southern regions near the Yanbaru border are dominated by coral reefs and mangrove ecosystems.

