==Economy==
[[File:Miyagi Buta Farm.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A Miyagi-buta heritage black pig at a certified breeding facility near Tatsugo.]]

Miyagi Prefecture possesses a robust and highly diversified economy that leverages its unique equatorial-maritime climate and its strategic position at the northern frontier of Okaiken. Historically reliant on [[w:Subsistence agriculture|subsistence farming]] and coastal [[w:Fishing|fishing]], the prefectural economy has rapidly modernized over the last half-century. Today, it operates as a vital economic bridge between the central Sanese mainland and the sovereign state of Yanbaru. The prefecture generates a significant portion of the national [[w:Gross domestic product|gross domestic product]] through a specialized blend of premium agriculture, advanced maritime logistics, high-tech manufacturing, and domestic tourism.

===Agriculture and livestock===
[[File:Red Emperor Mango Miyagi.jpg|thumb|left|200px|Red Emperor mangoes cultivated in a protected glasshouse facility near Amami.]]

The agricultural sector remains a cultural and economic cornerstone for the prefecture, heavily defined by the region's intense summer heat, high annual precipitation, and fertile [[w:Volcanic soil|volcanic soils]]. Because the climate ranges from [[w:Humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical]] to [[w:Tropical rainforest climate|tropical rainforest]], the prefecture avoids the killing [[w:Frost|frosts]] that limit agricultural growing seasons in the southern reaches of the archipelago.

The most famous and lucrative export of the region is '''Miyagi-buta''', a premium heritage breed of [[w:Black pig|black pig]]. Similar to the highly prized heritage pork of other global agricultural centers, Miyagi-buta is renowned for its exceptional [[w:Marbled meat|marbling]], tender texture, and distinctively sweet flavor profile. The prefectural agricultural [[w:Agricultural cooperative|cooperative]] maintains incredibly strict regulations on the rearing process to protect the brand's international prestige. The pigs are fed a specialized, locally sourced diet consisting primarily of fermented [[w:Sweet potato|sweet potatoes]], distilled grain mash, and [[w:Sugarcane|sugarcane]] byproducts. This highly controlled agricultural loop has made Miyagi-buta one of Okaiken's most sought-after luxury food exports, frequently featured in high-end culinary establishments across Adonia.

In addition to livestock, the prefecture's prolonged hot season enables the mass cultivation of tropical and subtropical fruits. Miyagi is the undisputed domestic leader in [[w:Mango|mango]] production. The most exclusive local [[w:Cultivar|cultivar]], known as the '''Red Emperor mango''', is cultivated in specialized glasshouses to protect the fruit from the torrential summer typhoons. These mangoes are sold at premium auction prices and frequently purchased as high-status corporate gifts. The region also produces massive commercial yields of [[w:Papaya|papayas]], [[w:Passion fruit (fruit)|passionfruit]], [[w:Lychee|lychees]], and [[w:Citrus|citrus]], which are rapidly transported to the central metropolitan markets via the high-speed rail network.

The prefecture is also a major producer of sweet potatoes and sugarcane, which form the base ingredients for the region's thriving beverage industry. The [[w:Distillation|distillation]] of these crops produces a highly potent, traditional Sanese [[w:Liquor|spirit]] that serves as a major domestic export and a cultural staple of the northern coastal communities.

===Maritime and aquaculture===
[[File:Shinagawa Port.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The deep-water port of Shinagawa, handling cargo traffic between Okaiken and Yanbaru.]]

Because Miyagi borders the treacherous straits separating Okaiken from Yanbaru, its maritime economy is highly developed. The capital city of Shinagawa and the northern coastal cities operate as critical deep-water logistics hubs. These ports facilitate the massive flow of imported raw materials from the Shendan Ocean and export finished manufactured goods back to the Fosian mainland.

The commercial fishing fleet operating out of Miyagi is one of the largest in the nation. The warm [[w:Ocean current|oceanic currents]] funneling through the strait create highly productive fishing grounds. The prefecture is a leading supplier of [[w:Skipjack tuna|skipjack tuna]], [[w:Yellowtail amberjack|yellowtail]], and [[w:Japanese amberjack|Japanese amberjack]]. In recent decades, the prefectural government has heavily subsidized the transition from open-ocean trawling to sustainable [[w:Aquaculture|aquaculture]]. The sheltered, deep-water bays along the rugged western coast have been converted into massive sea-farming grids, cultivating high-value amberjack and producing world-renowned [[w:Cultured pearl|cultured pearls]] that are exported to luxury jewelers globally.

===Manufacturing and technology===
[[File:Silicon Strait Miyagi.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Aerial view of the "Silicon Strait" industrial corridor near Shinagawa.]]

While agriculture and fishing form the traditional base of the economy, the manufacturing sector has seen explosive growth, earning the coastal corridor around Shinagawa the nickname "'''The Silicon Strait'''" ({{nihongo|シリコン海峡|Shirikon Kaikyō}}). Taking inspiration from the rapid industrialization of southern regions, the prefectural government established massive tax incentives to attract high-tech industries that require large amounts of affordable land and proximity to international shipping lanes.

The prefecture has become a specialized hub for maritime [[w:Robotics|robotics]] and [[w:Renewable energy|renewable energy]] research. Several major Sanese engineering firms operate research and development campuses in Miyagi, utilizing the rough, fast-moving currents of the Yanbaru strait to test deep-sea mining [[w:Submersible|submersibles]] and [[w:Tidal power|tidal turbine]] generators. Furthermore, the presence of the Harujima Shinkansen terminus has spurred the development of advanced [[w:Transit-oriented development|transit-oriented]] manufacturing, with several facilities producing specialized [[w:Microelectronics|microelectronics]] and [[w:Semiconductor|semiconductor]] components that are shipped overnight to the tech conglomerates in Okami.

===Tourism and services===
[[File:Miyagi Beach Resort.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A luxury beach resort near Yoron during the peak summer season.]]

The [[w:Service sector|service]] and tourism sectors have grown exponentially, driven by the prefecture's reputation as a premier domestic vacation destination. The hot, humid climate makes Miyagi one of the most popular beach and resort locations in Okaiken, particularly for residents of the colder, central mainland cities seeking an equatorial escape.

The coastline is dotted with luxury resorts, surfing enclaves, and historical sites featuring preserved pirate coves from the Sengoku period. The completion of the Harujima Shinkansen has dramatically accelerated this economic sector, cutting travel times from the capital and allowing for massive weekend tourism surges. The influx of domestic and international visitors has created a booming secondary economy of hospitality services, [[w:Boutique|boutique]] retail, and [[w:Culinary tourism|culinary tourism]] centered entirely around the consumption of fresh local seafood and premium Miyagi-buta.

==Transportation==
[[File:Shinagawa Shinkansen.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A Harujima Shinkansen train arriving at Shinagawa Station, the northern terminus of the line.]]

Miyagi Prefecture serves as a major transit hub connecting the northern coastal regions to the central Sanese mainland. It is crucially served by the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] high-speed rail network, which makes two major stops within the prefecture's borders:

* '''[[Shin-Sendai Station]]''' – An intermediate stop serving the city of Sendai and surrounding areas
* '''[[Shinagawa Station]]''' – The line's final northern terminus, serving the prefectural capital

The prefecture is also connected to the national expressway network, with the '''Miyagi Expressway''' running north-south along the coastal plain. The deep-water port of Shinagawa handles both cargo and passenger ferry services, with regular connections to ports in [[Yanbaru]].

